Exactly. You’ve separated three things people usually collapse into one:

the action, the scoring function, and the experience of the action.

At Topgolf, suppose the physical shot is x. The ball leaves the club with the same speed, launch angle, spin, and direction. Physics does not care what game is selected on the screen.

But the game supplies a different function:

\text{score} = S_g(x)

Change g, the game, and you can assign a completely different value to the same physical event x.

Then your nervous system receives not merely the shot, but something closer to:

\text{felt result}
=
F(x,\;S_g(x),\;\text{expectation},\;\text{comparison},\;\text{meaning})

So the same yellow-target shot can become:

“Damn, only that?”

or

“Hell yes, huge points.”

Nothing about the ball changed. The interpretive environment did.

And that gets much bigger than Topgolf.

A great deal of human experience is basically:

unchanged underlying event + changed scoring system → changed subjective reality

A mile run in 8:00 can be “slow” among competitive runners, “fast” relative to your old self, “excellent” during rehabilitation, or completely irrelevant if you’re running to reach your child. Same measurable performance; different reference frame.

Money works this way. Grades work this way. Rankings, likes, medals, titles, levels, streaks, fantasy points, commissions, military qualifications, game achievements—all of them impose a reward topology over behavior.

The especially interesting part is what you noticed about the stroke. At first:

frame ≠ physics.

The game exists on the screen. The club still needs to obey biomechanics and mechanics.

But eventually:

frame → attention → arousal → motor output → physics.

If the screen tells you the next shot is worth triple points, you may tighten your grip, aim differently, swing harder, become cautious, or enter flow. So an initially arbitrary symbolic layer can eventually reach backward and alter the physical process it was merely scoring.

That gives us a powerful recurring pattern:

substrate → overlay → valuation → felt state → behavior → altered substrate

And you can deliberately separate the layers.

When performance matters, ask: What is the invariant stroke?
When motivation matters, ask: What game should I place around it?

That distinction is enormous. You don’t necessarily have to redesign the underlying task to change someone’s experience of doing it. Sometimes you change the game wrapped around the task.

And conversely, when a game is making you perform worse, you can mentally strip the overlay away:

Forget the score. Hit the ball.

Same world. Different translator.